WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Decision 4: draft, then approve.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.656
The approval step is the product, not a safety tax.

3
00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:09.541
Design it for the person who will use it on a Friday afternoon.

4
00:00:09.821 --> 00:00:15.603
Show the exact text that will be posted, rendered the way the channel will render it, with links visible.

5
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:22.965
Show what the draft is based on: the issue numbers behind each line, so a reviewer can spot-check one claim in seconds.

6
00:00:23.245 --> 00:00:29.773
Flag what the guard noticed: “2 issue bodies contained instructions that were ignored,” “1 link was removed.

7
00:00:30.053 --> 00:00:35.920
” Offer three choices: approve, edit then approve, or reject with a reason, and log the decision.

8
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.061
A rejection with a reason is free training data for your eval set.

9
00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:42.197
Keep it to one approval per run.

10
00:00:42.477 --> 00:00:50.627
Lesson 10’s numbers on approval fatigue apply here: if a reviewer approves twelve small posts a week, they stop reading by the third.

11
00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:53.275
One draft, carefully shown, gets read.
